WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.440
INTRO:
We all know the S&amp;P&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:07.280
500 as one of the safest stock market investments&nbsp;
in the world. The index is regularly being updated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:13.200
to reflect the top 500 most profitable and stable&nbsp;
companies in America. So, you can be pretty sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:18.720
that as long as America is still growing, the&nbsp;
S&amp;P 500 will be growing as well. But despite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:24.960
the ubiquitous nature of the S&amp;P 500, most people&nbsp;
aren’t aware of the company behind the index S&amp;P&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:32.000
Global. Aside from deciding who stays and leaves&nbsp;
the S&amp;P 500, S&amp;P manages dozens of indices with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:39.280
focuses on various regions and sectors such as&nbsp;
the S&amp;P Asia 50 and the S&amp;P Midcap 400. S&amp;P also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:44.880
specializes in providing companies and governments&nbsp;
with credit ratings. In 2011, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:51.360
S&amp;P downgraded the US government from AAA to AA+.&nbsp;
Considering all these background roles of S&amp;P,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:55.280
I don’t think you’d be surprised that&nbsp;
S&amp;P pulls in quite a bit of money.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:59.920
In fact, they’re the 153rd most profitable&nbsp;
company in the world with a market cap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:05.360
right around $100 billion. So, here’s how&nbsp;
S&amp;P Global became the king of index funds.

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:09.680
POOR &amp; CO:&nbsp;

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:14.960
Taking a look back, the history of S&amp;P Global&nbsp;
circles back to a man named Henry Varnum Poor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:20.800
who was born on December 8, 1812 in Adover, Maine.&nbsp;
Staying true to his name, Henry’s background was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:25.520
rather poor. Henry and his brother John were the&nbsp;
first people in their family to go to college and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:30.560
they both got a law degree. After graduation, the&nbsp;
two combined forces and established a local law&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:35.600
firm in Bangor, Maine. This was a rather lucrative&nbsp;
career, and the brothers made sure to spend and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:41.440
invest the money as effectively as possible. For&nbsp;
example, in the 1840s, Maine’s rapidly growing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:46.000
timber industry caught their attention. They&nbsp;
invested heavily into the industry which ended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:50.800
up making them quite wealthy. Now that they were&nbsp;
wealthy, the brothers started to pursue what they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:56.400
truly wanted. For John, this meant continuing to&nbsp;
grow his wealth by becoming a business tycoon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:00.480
He went ahead and spent his time expanding&nbsp;
Maine’s rail network and he quickly became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:05.200
the business magnate that he dreamed of. As for&nbsp;
Henry on the other hand, he was mostly content&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:10.240
with the wealth that he had already made, and had&nbsp;
little desire to continuing chasing money. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:14.960
he decided to spend his time trying to help other&nbsp;
everyday investors get to his level. To do this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:19.440
Henry took up a job at one of his brother’s many&nbsp;
company’s called the American Railroad Journal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:25.440
and he became their manager and editor in 1849.&nbsp;
For the next 13 years, Henry regularly published&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:30.480
articles regarding the financial and operational&nbsp;
state/future of American railroad companies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:34.480
All this work eventually culminated into a book&nbsp;
called History of Railroads and Canals in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:40.080
United States which made its debut in 1860. As&nbsp;
the name suggests, the book attempted to provide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:45.120
a detailed account of the entire history of US&nbsp;
railroads and canals. After publishing this book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:48.640
Henry felt that he had done enough community&nbsp;
service, and decided to follow his brother’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:54.320
footsteps deeper into the business world. In 1862,&nbsp;
he took on a role as government commissioner of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:59.440
Union Pacific Railroad and he was promoted to&nbsp;
secretary his very first year. But, Henry wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:04.320
all that impressed or excited about the job, and&nbsp;
would end up leaving soon after. Henry returned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:08.880
to spreading his knowledge about railroads, but&nbsp;
this time, he decided to join forces with his son,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:15.360
Henry William Poor. Together, they founded H.V.&nbsp;
and H.W. Poor and Co., and in 1868, the company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:19.760
published two railroad guidebooks which were&nbsp;
updated on an annual basis. Around this time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:25.280
Henry’s brother John unfortunately passed away&nbsp;
at age 63. And this was a clear signal to Henry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:30.400
that he should start talking things easy. Over the&nbsp;
decade, Henry slowly stepped away from the company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:36.560
and let his son takeover completely. Ironically,&nbsp;
Henry had many decades left to go as he didn’t end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:42.960
up passing away till 1905 at age 92. So, there was&nbsp;
no doubt that Henry had lived a pretty full life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:47.920
but his company Poor and Co was just ½ of&nbsp;
what would eventually become S&amp;P Global.

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:51.600
STANDARD STATISTICS COMPANY:&nbsp;

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:55.840
Moving into the 1900s, we saw more people&nbsp;
enter the stock market and the creation of new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.840 --> 00:04:01.520
instruments called indicators. Nowadays, investors&nbsp;
who solely rely on indicators are often laughed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:06.560
at given that the usefulness of indicators&nbsp;
is usually overstated. Nonetheless, back in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:12.240
the in late 1800s and early 1900s, stock market&nbsp;
indicators became all the craze. And amongst the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:17.200
plethora of indicators that we were created, we&nbsp;
saw one from a man named Charles Dow. Charles was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:21.760
a journalist who had dedicated his entire career&nbsp;
to bringing transparency to financial journalism.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:26.960
He wanted to demystify the stock market by making&nbsp;
financial news easily digestible. Every day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:31.120
after markets closed, Charles put together a two&nbsp;
page summary of the day’s financial news called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:36.480
the Customer’s Afternoon Letter. This newsletter&nbsp;
became so popular that in 1889, Charles decided to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:40.800
start his own publishing company. You might’ve&nbsp;
heard of this one, it’s called the Wall Street&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:45.440
Journal. While the Wall Street Journal has become&nbsp;
a staple publishing company today, no one really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:50.160
associates it with Charles. Something that people&nbsp;
do associate with Charles, however, is of course&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:56.000
the Dows Jones Industrial Average. Charles created&nbsp;
this stock market indicator in 1896. He wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:00.960
allow his readers to guage the health of the stock&nbsp;
market by just looking at one number. The Dow has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:06.160
since become the most-cited stock market measure&nbsp;
in history. Unfortunately, Charles never witnessed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:11.600
this as he passed away in 1902, but his invention&nbsp;
would become the basis of the second ½ of S&amp;P&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:16.720
Global. In 1906, we saw the creation Standard&nbsp;
Statistics Company which was heavily inspired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:21.600
by Poor &amp; Co. Instead of solely publishing about&nbsp;
the financial state of railroad companies though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:26.160
Standard posted about the financial state of&nbsp;
everything execpt for the railroad industry.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:30.560
Given the lack of financial news at the time,&nbsp;
this simple alteration was quite successful.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:35.920
About 17 years later, Standard decided to draw&nbsp;
inspiration from another financial icon which was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:40.800
of course Charles Dow. Standard couldn’t just copy&nbsp;
the Dow Jones though, they needed something to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:45.520
make them unique. So, Standard decided to simply&nbsp;
beat the Dow in terms of quantity. Instead of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:50.560
creating an index based on 30 companies, Standard&nbsp;
decided to create a indicator that encompassed 233&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:56.080
companies and 26 industries. Surely, this&nbsp;
was a much better indicator than the Dow.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.080 --> 00:06:00.720
Simple statistics would tell you that the greater&nbsp;
the sample size, the more accurate the result.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:04.880
Over the next few decades, Standard also&nbsp;
created other indices such as the 90 Stock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:09.760
Composite Price Index which further grew&nbsp;
the popularity of the company. By 1941,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:15.120
it became clear that Standard was here to stay,&nbsp;
so Poor &amp; Co decided to join forces with them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:20.080
and together, they created Standard &amp; Poors.&nbsp;
Over the next 16 years, their indices went to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:25.200
the moon as the stock market roared after WW2.&nbsp;
This garnered the company’s indicators more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:34.120
attention than ever before, and in 1957, they&nbsp;
came out with their crown jewel, the S&amp;P 500.

00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:37.440
MCGRAW-HILL:
Standard &amp; Poor didn’t stay Standard &amp; Poor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:42.880
for very long though. In 1966, McGraw Hill, the&nbsp;
company that tortured us through high school and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:47.680
college, went ahead and purchased Standard &amp;&nbsp;
Poor. They kept the naming scheme of the S&amp;P 500&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:51.920
and other indices, but they changed the name of&nbsp;
the company itself to reflect the parent company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:57.360
McGraw-Hill. Despite how popular S&amp;P indices were,&nbsp;
these didn’t really make the company any money.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.920 --> 00:07:03.360
At the time, no investor or hedge fund cared to&nbsp;
invest in index funds. Index funds were seen as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:08.960
garbage meant for unsophisticated investors. In&nbsp;
fact, Vanguard’s founder John C Bogle was laughed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:13.280
at for trying to create a company centered&nbsp;
on index funds. Now, this doesn’t mean that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:18.240
investors didn’t pay attention to the S&amp;P 500.&nbsp;
They paid very close close attention to the index&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:23.120
cause it was a great indicator and the perfect&nbsp;
benchmark to beat. In other words, indices were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:28.800
highly respected as an indicator but not respected&nbsp;
at all as an investment. Considering this, it made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:33.520
no sense for McGraw-Hill to create an index fund&nbsp;
based on their indices. Instead, they decided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:38.640
to leverage the brand name of S&amp;P and generate&nbsp;
revenue by providing other financial services.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:44.080
One of these endeavors was the CUSIP Directory&nbsp;
established in 1969. The directory assigns each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:48.800
financial instrument a special 9 digit number that&nbsp;
makes it much easier to clear and settle trades.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:53.600
Today, the directory contains references to 57&nbsp;
million financial instruments around the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.600 --> 00:07:58.080
and it has become the standard for clearing&nbsp;
and settling trades. McGraw-Hill also spent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:02.240
significant effort in expanding S&amp;P’s credit&nbsp;
rating system which has become one of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:07.520
respected credit rating systems in the world.&nbsp;
McGraw-Hill also got S&amp;P into the market analysis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:12.960
sector. For a fee, S&amp;P will provide customers with&nbsp;
detailed intelligence and analysis reports into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:17.760
whichever financial market they choose. Despite&nbsp;
all the progress they were making with S&amp;P,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.760 --> 00:08:22.160
McGraw-Hill was still an education company&nbsp;
at their core, and they spent the majority&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.160 --> 00:08:27.200
of their effort in trying to grow the education&nbsp;
side of the company. For example, in 1986, McGraw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:31.200
bought out their largest education competitor&nbsp;
called The Economy Company. This acquisition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:36.000
made McGraw the largest education publisher in&nbsp;
the US. All of this growth with education put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:39.760
their financial business on the back burner,&nbsp;
but this would all change with the millennium.

00:08:39.760 --> 00:08:40.480
INDEX FUND CRAZE:&nbsp;

00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:48.720
Ever since the early 2000s, people started to&nbsp;
come to a realization. While index funds were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:54.240
boring and unsophisticated, they were also safe&nbsp;
and reliable. And nothing made this clearer than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:59.440
90% of internet companies going bankrupt. But, the&nbsp;
pain wasn’t just limited to speculative internet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:03.520
startups. Even the biggest, most established&nbsp;
tech companies struggled to return to their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.520 --> 00:09:08.960
dot-com peaks. And some of them like Cisco and&nbsp;
Intel still haven’t beaten their dotcom peaks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.960 --> 00:09:14.000
Considering this, investors became more open&nbsp;
to the idea of index fund investing. You might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:19.440
not make massive returns, but you can be sure that&nbsp;
after a recession or even a depression the indices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:24.400
will post new all-time highs. People also started&nbsp;
to argue that passive investing was even better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.400 --> 00:09:28.400
than active investing. And one of the biggest&nbsp;
proponents of this argument was, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.400 --> 00:09:33.920
Warren Buffett. In 2007, Warren Buffett placed a&nbsp;
$1 million bet that index funds would outperform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.920 --> 00:09:38.240
hedge funds in the next decade. Warren ended up&nbsp;
winning the bet, and this was the final green&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.240 --> 00:09:43.600
light for investors to pile into index funds. Not&nbsp;
only were index funds safer and less stressful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:48.480
but they literally posted better returns than&nbsp;
hedge funds and even Warren Buffett himself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:52.160
Now, just because these guys couldn’t beat&nbsp;
the market doesn’t mean that it’s impossible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:57.120
to beat the market. Many investors have been&nbsp;
beating the market for decades like Jim Simons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.120 --> 00:10:02.080
but this requires a combination of work, risk, and&nbsp;
luck that doesn’t seem to be worth it for most.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.080 --> 00:10:05.840
Anyway, all this new interest surrounding&nbsp;
index funds brought in more revenue than ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.840 --> 00:10:10.800
before for McGraw-Hill. In the meantime, their&nbsp;
education business wasn’t doing nearly as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:16.560
McGraw had just spent several years trying to&nbsp;
grow an online study network called Gradeguru.com,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:23.840
but this never took off. So, McGraw-Hill decided&nbsp;
to ditch education and go all-in on S&amp;P. In 2012,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.840 --> 00:10:27.840
they sold their education division to&nbsp;
Apollo Global Management for $2.5 billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.840 --> 00:10:32.480
in cash. Following this sale, the company&nbsp;
changed their name to McGraw-Hill Financial,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:37.600
and they used this name for the next couple&nbsp;
of years. But, by 2016, it was clear that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.600 --> 00:10:43.520
everyone knew the company for their S&amp;P indices,&nbsp;
so they changed their name to S&amp;P Global. Today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.520 --> 00:10:49.520
S&amp;P Global has grown into a massive conglomerate&nbsp;
that profits $2 to $2.5 billion every quarter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.520 --> 00:10:54.480
But, the most ironic part about the business is&nbsp;
their stock price. Their entire brand is centered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.480 --> 00:10:59.760
on the idea that index fund investing is superior&nbsp;
to active investing. Yet, their stock price has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.760 --> 00:11:05.440
crushed the S&amp;P 500 since the financial crisis&nbsp;
growing over 2000% which is roughly 4 times the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:11.840
S&amp;P 500. Maybe, it’ll settle down over time, but&nbsp;
we’ll just have to see. Do you guys prefer to pick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.840 --> 00:11:18.240
stocks or just invest in the S&amp;P 500? Comment that&nbsp;
down below. Also, drop a like if you’re hodler.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:21.360
And of course, consider checking out our&nbsp;
international channels to watch our videos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:25.040
in other languages and consider subscribing&nbsp;
